# Target C — hostile audit of Section 126 outer-tooth exactness

**Date:** 2026-09-12
**Base theorem state:** `2026-09-12.audit.46`
**Artifact:** `research/target_c_section126_outer_tooth_exactness.md`
**Verdict:** **PASS at proof-only Type-II scope.**
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

The audit attacks the only delicate point: whether a two-edge interval in the
occurrence-counted global outer walk is really the same object as a bounded triangular
face in one augmented harmonic cell.

## 1. The Section-63 interval is the pocket-side interval

Canonical Section 63 chooses distinguished exterior occurrences of the loaded vertices
in cyclic force-hull order and partitions one outer-face boundary walk into intervals

\[
W_i:b_i\leadsto b_{i+1}.
\]

Canonical Section 67 adds every missing global hull side `b_i b_{i+1}`. Each such side
lies on `partial F`, has no positive-core vertex in its open segment, and creates one
bounded convex pocket on its inward side. Since the other side of the added edge is the
exterior face, the boundary of that pocket minus the added edge is precisely the old
outer-face interval between the corresponding consecutive distinguished loaded
occurrences. Thus the pocket boundary is

\[
b_i b_{i+1}\cup W_i.
\]

This remains true with bridges and repeated articulation occurrences: those repetitions
are part of `W_i` with multiplicity. In particular, if `ell_i=2`, the new pocket is the
simple triangle bounded by the two old contact edges and the added hull side.

## 2. The middle vertex of a two-edge Type-II tooth is unloaded

Write `W_i=b_i-x-b_{i+1}` with `s_i>1`. If `x` were loaded, both unit edges would be
loaded-loaded contacts. Audited proof-only Section 124 says every such contact is a
global loaded-hull side. Therefore `x` would be a cyclic neighbor of both
`b_i,b_{i+1}`, while those two vertices are themselves cyclic neighbors. A simple
convex polygon can have this adjacency pattern only for `q=3`.

Canonical Section 59 has `h/D` bounded below by `2rho-o(1)` and `q>=h`, so `q>3` for
all sufficiently large Type-II survivors. Hence `x` is unloaded.

Its two contacts put it in one unloaded component `I_r` with both endpoints in
`A_r`. Since `b_i,b_{i+1}` are consecutive in the full cyclic set `B`, they are
consecutive in the subset `A_r`; their chord is a side of `P_r`.

The global pocket triangle contains no other positive-core vertex by definition of a
face of `Ghat`. Therefore it is also a bounded face of the induced augmented graph
`J_r^+`. This verifies the forward map from `ell_i=2` to an augmented triangle without
requiring any unstated occurrence injectivity.

## 3. An augmented triangle is globally exposed

Proof-only Section 125 already establishes that every augmented triangular face has
one unloaded apex `x`, two old unit contacts, and a nonunit base `ab` that is a side of
the **global** loaded hull. The only possible concern is that the triangle could be a
face of `J_r^+` but contain positive-core material from another component, so that it
would not be the global pocket incident to `ab`.

That cannot occur. The apex belongs to `I_r`; positivity/harmonic trapping with at
least three noncollinear loaded attachments puts every unloaded vertex of `I_r` in
`int(P_r)`. Hence the open triangular face lies in `int(P_r)`. If a vertex of another
unloaded component lay in this open triangle, its harmonic cell would contain that
vertex in its interior and would have interior intersection with `P_r`, contradicting
canonical Section 83. A loaded vertex cannot lie there because loaded vertices are
extreme vertices of `F`. Planarity then excludes a full-core edge crossing the open
triangle without a vertex.

Thus after the global base `ab` is added, the triangle is the global `Ghat` pocket on
that side. Removing the added base leaves exactly the old two-edge outer interval.
Therefore every augmented triangle gives `ell_i=2`.

## 4. Injection is exact

Different global hull sides are different indices `i`. Section 125 already proves that
two distinct augmented triangular faces cannot charge the same global hull side. The
forward and reverse constructions both preserve the base side, so they are inverse
bijections. Hence

\[
T=\#\{i:s_i>1,\ \ell_i=2\}.
\]

## 5. The `u_B` inequality is pure exact algebra

From canonical Section 63,

\[
x_B=\sum_i(\ell_i-1),
\qquad
u_B=x_B-m.
\]

Splitting unit/nonunit hull sides gives

\[
u_B
=\sum_{s_i>1}(\ell_i-2)
 +\sum_{s_i=1}(\ell_i-1).
\]

All summands are nonnegative. Every nonunit index not counted by `T` has
`ell_i>=3`, hence contributes at least one to the first sum. Therefore

\[
0\le m-T\le u_B.
\]

No assumption that a unit hull side has `ell_i=1` is needed.

## 6. Regressions and scope

- A bridge excursion is allowed and only increases `ell_i`/`u_B`.
- Several two-edge teeth may share an unloaded apex; the theorem counts hull bases, not
  apices, so this causes no collision.
- A tooth base may approach length two or length one; no uniform metric gap is claimed.
- The theorem uses Type-II Section 124. It says nothing about the macroscopic-container
  branch.
- Contact-isolated source points remain present and irrelevant to the positive outer
  walk.

**Audit verdict:** PASS at proof-only Type-II scope. Section 126 sharpens the structural
meaning of `u_B` but is not a C-II contradiction.
